  "query_preview": "..\n(Thursday night through Tuesday)\nIssued at 122 AM EDT Wed Sep 23 2026\n\nFor the end of the week and through this weekend, a frontal boundary \nwill push southwards in tandem with the upper level trough \nstrengthening off the Atlantic coast and the front will eventually \nsettle over far southern Florida or the Florida Straits this \nweekend. As the front stalls, much drier air will advect into \nCentral Florida, with some of it expected to push into portions of \nour region, mainly the lake region. Near the lake and perhaps parts \nof northern Broward/Hendry, PWATs have potential to fall to 1.2-1.4 \ninches, which would be well below average. For far southern Florida, \nthese values could still fall to 1.6-1.8 inches, which is around \naverage for this time of year. With this anticipation of less \nmoisture available and a thicker dry layer in the mid levels \nidentified on model soundings, shower and thunderstorm coverage is \nexpected to decrease for all areas. Lowest PoPs are forecast for the \nlake region where the driest air will be located, with higher PoPs \nfurther south. This is reflected with PoPs under 10-20% this weekend \nnear the lake and 30-50% for the southern half of the forecast area. \nDew points are projected to fall into the upper 60s for most areas \nacross the region with this frontal passage this weekend, but it \nwill still feel rather muggy outside.\n\nDaily high temperatures each day will continue to reach the upper \n80s to near 90 each day with overnight lows mostly in the low to mid \n70s. However, areas near the lake could see temperatures drop into \nthe mid to upper 60s for several nights beginning Friday night.",
